# Cross-repo siblings cloned by setup-dev-env. They are io-mon's `uses:`
# library producers in repro.nim (reprobuild builds each from source and
# threads its `src/` root onto io-mon's compile); they are also the `--path`
# siblings config.nims resolves for the plain `nim c` / `nimble` build.
#
# WHY THESE CARRY EXPLICIT SHAs RATHER THAN RESOLVING FROM THE WORKSPACE LOCK
#
# A bare entry makes `clone-siblings.sh` ask metacraft-manifests for the
# workspace lock of the io-mon commit under test, and a missing lock is FATAL:
#
#   ::error::No workspace lock for io-mon (candidates: ...). Neither a
#   repo-workspaces locks/<project>/io-mon/<sha>.xml nor a reprobuild
#   locks/<project>/io-mon/<sha>.toml exists in
#   metacraft-labs/metacraft-manifests@latest.
#
# ...before a single dependency is provisioned. That is not hypothetical here.
# Enumerated over every `ci-reprobuild.yml` run in this repository's history
# (73 runs, hence 73 linux-arm64 jobs): 40 of those jobs failed in `Setup dev
# env` with exactly that message and had `repro build` marked `skipped`, 32
# never started at all, and exactly ONE ever reached `repro build`. The lock is
# written by a post-commit/pre-push hook that REFUSES to write one whenever any
# unrelated sibling in the developer's workspace has uncommitted changes, so
# whole stretches of io-mon commits carry no lock at all and every lane on them
# is dead before it compiles anything.
#
# With no bare entry left, nothing needs a revision FROM the lock, so
# `lock_unavailable()` degrades to a `::warning::` and the clones proceed.
#
# THIS IS THE FALLBACK, NOT THE GOAL, and `clone-siblings.sh` prescribes that
# order: (1) declare these repos in io-mon's project manifest in
# metacraft-labs/metacraft-manifests so the lock pins them for every future
# commit with nothing to maintain; (2) only where (1) is impossible, name a
# 40-hex commit SHA here -- a commit, never a branch, because a branch tip is
# exactly the unpinned resolve the mechanism exists to prevent. Delete the
# `=<sha>` suffixes as soon as (1) lands.
#
# Both SHAs below are reachable from their repository's `origin/dev`
# (`git branch -r --contains <sha>`) and are the revisions the io-mon
# workspace that produced the aarch64 shim fix was built against.
nim-stackable-hooks=f3367d44b668e16e9eaa42dc77e51b2e6f5e5954  # on metacraft-labs/nim-stackable-hooks@dev
nim-shm-queue=5a8e43b52fa202859658692c9f8432967f3971ea  # on metacraft-labs/nim-shm-queue@dev

# nim-shm-gset is required by `src/io_mon/writer.nim` (`import
# shm_gset/transport`) and by scripts/build_shim.sh, but it was missing from
# this list, so `just build` failed with "missing nim-shm-gset at
# ../nim-shm-gset/src" and `just test` with "cannot open file:
# shm_gset/transport". Both were reported as PASSING jobs, because those
# steps pipe into `tee` and the default `run` shell has no pipefail; ci.yml
# now sets `shell: bash` so the exit status survives.
#
# Pinned to an explicit revision rather than resolved from the workspace
# lock: the io-mon locks published to metacraft-manifests (8b6d0b9b,
# ea5fa77a) list only io-mon, nim-stackable-hooks and nim-shm-queue, so a
# bare `nim-shm-gset` entry would fail resolution instead of cloning. The
# durable fix is to add nim-shm-gset to the io-mon workspace manifest so it
# is locked alongside its siblings; this pin should be dropped then.
# cf0adf2f is metacraft-labs/nim-shm-gset@dev, the revision io-mon's build
# and full test suite are green against.
nim-shm-gset=cf0adf2f39647f5b203b2ab694414a3c50e93951
